{"w":2400,"h":3170,"lines":[{"c":1,"y":0.048733101689458214,"t":"Audioscripts","x":0.10157085446076858,"w":0.11739571889241537,"h":0.017359377984368818},{"x":0.10351386703048504,"t":"TUTOR:","y":0.099903288560267312,"w":0.05016996383666994,"h":0.010949236511808125,"c":1},{"x":0.10364683260018515,"c":1,"t":"MARTIN.","y":0.14970930220506284,"w":0.053742802937825518,"h":0.0087209304424490375},{"w":0.62390075683593749,"y":0.097133928692654248,"t":"In the last section, relevance to the present day, you've got some good ideas but","x":0.19574056332022829,"h":0.015869009457176064,"c":1},{"t":"this is where you need to move away from the ideas of others and give your own","h":0.013081394761142673,"y":0.11482558168552159,"w":0.62188100179036454,"x":0.19769673510839766,"c":1},{"t":". Q30","w":0.047984644571940072,"x":0.82149712103350114,"y":0.11482558165285894,"c":1,"h":0.013081394761142562},{"y":0.13226744170523796,"c":1,"w":0.078694820404052762,"x":0.19577734978130526,"t":"Viewpoint.","h":0.01162790725659868},{"x":0.19769673454025713,"t":"OK, we'll think about that. Now shall we...","y":0.14668769722627295,"w":0.32821497599283855,"h":0.01324921135646695,"c":1},{"t":"SECTION 4","y":0.19767441855186996,"x":0.10364683294187266,"w":0.14971209208170572,"c":1,"h":0.015988372176979682},{"w":0.69097890218098956,"y":0.2267441860001167,"c":1,"x":0.10172743949709172,"h":0.015988372176979682,"t":"In this series of lectures about the history of weather forecasting, I'Il start by examining its"},{"t":"early history - that ll be the subject of today's talk.","x":0.10172744392778602,"y":0.2441640381168857,"h":0.014556891985872289,"w":0.38771593729654952,"c":1},{"x":0.1036468227641988,"t":"OK, so we'll start by going back thousands of years. Most ancient cultures had weather gods,","y":0.26744186042091744,"w":0.71976969401041668,"h":0.015988372176979571,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.016105166895532541,"x":0.10359354816603088,"y":0.28246134124518618,"t":"and weather catastrophes, such as floods, played an important role in many creation myths.","w":0.71026280721028645},{"y":0.29936908487757119,"c":1,"x":0.1055662114949565,"t":"Generally, weather was attributed to the whims of the gods, as the wide range of weather","w":0.68522074381510423,"h":0.01324921135646695},{"w":0.68522074381510412,"t":"gods in various cultures shows. For instance, there's the Egyptian sun god Ra, and Thor,","x":0.10364682574519908,"h":0.014561477444525406,"y":0.31540697686675601,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"the Norse god of thunder and lightning. Many ancient civilisations developed rites such as","x":0.10172744756110366,"y":0.33139534879281607,"h":0.015988372176979571,"w":0.69481765747070312},{"y":0.34731861202972614,"t":"dances in order to make the weather gods look kindly on them.","w":0.48752400716145838,"h":0.01324921135646695,"x":0.1036468258258607,"c":1},{"y":0.34738372075610557,"t":"Q31","x":0.83493282165696092,"w":0.034548943837483637,"c":1,"h":0.01162790725659868},{"t":"But the weather was of daily importance: observing the skies and drawing the correct","y":0.3720930235477431,"x":0.10364684492534261,"h":0.013081394761142784,"w":0.65834930419921878,"c":1},{"t":"conclusions from these observations was really important, in fact their survival depended on","h":0.013249211356466839,"c":1,"w":0.71017272949218757,"y":0.38801261857425406,"x":0.10172745474723835},{"y":0.38808139560634736,"t":"Q32","x":0.83301343516757476,"w":0.036468331019083644,"h":0.013081394761142562,"c":1},{"x":0.10172745669734126,"w":0.68714009602864579,"h":0.016119506908139902,"t":"it. It isn't known when people first started to observe the skies, but at around 650 BC, the","y":0.40406976739733635,"c":1},{"w":0.69673706054687501,"h":0.014642175740624097,"t":"Babylonians produced the first short-range weather forecasts, based on their observations","x":0.10364682484952747,"y":0.42005813965558991,"c":1},{"x":0.10172744038451582,"w":0.67946258544921867,"c":1,"y":0.43596214496836394,"t":"of clouds and other phenomena. The Chinese also recognised weather patterns, and by","h":0.014619250252419946},{"w":0.68138193766276045,"t":"300 BC, astronomers had developed a calendar which divided the year into 24 festivals.","h":0.016103917792768541,"y":0.45203488367637323,"c":1,"x":0.10172745716298541},{"w":0.034548943837483637,"t":"Q33","y":0.4360465118832797,"x":0.83493282165696092,"h":0.013081394761142562,"c":1},{"w":0.034548943837483637,"c":1,"h":0.013081394761142562,"t":"Q34","x":0.83493282165696092,"y":0.45203488397630287},{"h":0.014525858160073124,"t":"each associated with a different weather phenomenon.","y":0.46707508769359984,"x":0.10357666384555141,"w":0.42435165405273434,"c":1},{"x":0.10172743989502057,"t":"The ancient Greeks were the first to develop a more scientific approach to explaining the","y":0.49273255826338702,"w":0.68522074381510423,"h":0.014534883469061177,"c":1},{"y":0.50872093035613308,"t":"weather. The work of the philosopher and scientist Aristotle, in the fourth century BC, is","w":0.67178502400716145,"h":0.014622918137992658,"c":1,"x":0.10364683531925001},{"w":0.70441457112630212,"y":0.52325581371165553,"t":"especially noteworthy, as his ideas held sway for nearly 2,000 years. In 340 BC, he wrote a","h":0.015988372176979682,"x":0.10172745769500627,"c":1},{"x":0.1017274374547307,"y":0.54069400605528806,"w":0.71401153564453124,"c":1,"h":0.014538551956321144,"t":"book in which he attempted to account for the formation of rain, clouds, wind and storms. He"},{"x":0.1017274480540208,"y":0.55668604663541865,"c":1,"w":0.68905949910481767,"h":0.014607329022621185,"t":"also described celestial phenomena such as haloes - that is, bright circles of light around"},{"c":1,"y":0.57255520522722758,"w":0.76967371622721348,"t":"the sun, the moon and bright stars - and comets. Many of his observations were surprisingly Q35","h":0.014654096970422748,"x":0.10172744122383601},{"x":0.10364682520966056,"h":0.014554141071692728,"w":0.69097890218098956,"c":1,"t":"accurate. For example, he believed that heat could cause water to evaporate. But he also","y":0.58864353321850549},{"w":0.70825337727864579,"h":0.014591739305562457,"c":1,"x":0.10172743794595306,"t":"jumped to quite a few wrong conclusions, such as that winds are breathed out by the Earth.","y":0.60465116291438215},{"h":0.016012191772460938,"t":"Errors like this were rectified from the Renaissance onwards.","y":0.61776919538653274,"w":0.47239161173502608,"x":0.1035509321392396,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.66711764259906792,"w":0.67192036946614575,"x":0.10164880656770464,"h":0.018923193272732064,"t":"For nearly 2,000 years, Aristotle's work was accepted as the chief authority on weather"},{"w":0.69865641276041668,"c":1,"x":0.10172745571085585,"t":"theory. Alongside this, though, in the Middle Ages weather observations were passed on in","h":0.016088328075709812,"y":0.68454258677655344},{"w":0.70057581583658846,"c":1,"y":0.70058139547239684,"h":0.014560560473132145,"x":0.10172744705360487,"t":"the form of proverbs, such as 'Red sky at night, shepherd's delight; red sky in the morning,"},{"x":0.83301343517353377,"t":"Q36","h":0.013081394761142673,"y":0.70058139560420984,"w":0.036468331019083644,"c":1},{"h":0.015963262564000269,"c":1,"x":0.10359546727359108,"t":"shepherd's warning'. Many of these are based on very good observations and are accurate,","y":0.71516643106746747,"w":0.70836227416992192},{"h":0.013249211356466839,"c":1,"x":0.1036468388395915,"t":"as contemporary meteorologists have discovered","y":0.73249211367172951,"w":0.38003837585449218},{"t":"For centuries, any attempt to forecast the weather could only be based on personal","x":0.10166998511528362,"c":1,"y":0.75578745971546946,"h":0.017269496737218448,"w":0.64501195271809897},{"y":0.77180232586548037,"t":"observations, but in the fifteenth century scientists began to see the need for instruments.","w":0.68905949910481767,"x":0.10364683352118675,"h":0.013081394761142673,"c":1},{"h":0.013203123315275622,"c":1,"t":"Q37","x":0.83297546811110079,"y":0.7717414614353818,"w":0.03654426574707037},{"c":1,"t":"Until then, the only ones available were weather vanes - to determine the wind direction -","x":0.10364684182548435,"h":0.013249211356466839,"w":0.69289825439453123,"y":0.78769716086421782},{"y":0.80078782591574571,"t":"and early versions of rain gauges. One of the first, invented in the fifteenth century, was a","x":0.10165425120835228,"w":0.68921376546223956,"c":1,"h":0.019174217801740889},{"h":0.014617416309633535,"t":"hygrometer, which measured humidity. This was one of many inventions that contributed to","y":0.81976744198375706,"x":0.10172744704070065,"w":0.70057581583658846,"c":1},{"y":0.83382242603248713,"t":"the development of weather forecasting.","x":0.10161401235476086,"w":0.30925272623697919,"h":0.015092410499741193,"c":1},{"h":0.016866900567376719,"x":0.10167192592867802,"w":0.76784723917643227,"t":"In 1592, the Italian scientist and inventor Galileo developed the world's first thermometer. His Q38","y":0.85884968093259595,"c":1},{"h":0.015988236195657479,"c":1,"t":"student Torricelli later invented the barometer, which allowed people to measure atmospheric","x":0.10166724374453338,"y":0.87475991791086682,"w":0.71796513875325518},{"y":0.89052988076360107,"w":0.66809092203776044,"h":0.017154031747523169,"t":"pressure. In 1648, the French philosopher Pascal proved that pressure decreases with","x":0.10356850871174288,"c":1},{"w":0.696876932779948,"c":1,"y":0.90668850360221931,"h":0.018354680635951692,"t":"altitude. This discovery was verified by English astronomer Halley in 1686; and Halley was","x":0.10165659459411595},{"h":0.015722532302423153,"x":0.10352849177444814,"y":0.92248508941663354,"c":1,"w":0.30927187601725259,"t":"also the first person to map trade winds."},{"y":0.95494186072048881,"x":0.10172744737673066,"h":0.013081394761142673,"c":1,"w":0.034548943837483734,"t":"106"}]}